                        "glosses": [
                            "A cliffside dwelling made in the living rock by humans."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "357f279a-249a-4489-bf43-d74241b43dda",
                                "sense_id": "74608287-e8e7-3f0b-9dfd-7c59fbb16e68",
                                "text": "Given the absence of other means of dating the cavates, ceramic dating was the focus of the data collection, but we also recorded vessel form."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "739cb0c2-267b-4c9a-92c8-bb628411a92f",
                                "sense_id": "74608287-e8e7-3f0b-9dfd-7c59fbb16e68",
                                "text": "The reader may wonder about the significance of the Ahuitzotl figure in a cavate near Los Alamos National Laboratory."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "33e74c7e-fd6c-4658-8f14-58c06e145306",
                                "sense_id": "74608287-e8e7-3f0b-9dfd-7c59fbb16e68",
                                "text": "While the cavate was originally carved out generations, if not centuries, before 1680, the ceramics associated with its final substantial occupation (including Kapo Black, Tewa Polychrome, and Glaze F pottery) have led investigators to conclude that Pueblo people reoccupied it sometime between 1680 and 1700."
                            }
                        ]
